#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049119 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049837 :topic/math-academy-id 6341 :topic/title "Finding Intersections of Lines and Quadratic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050473 :topic/math-academy-id 3562 :topic/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644 :topic/math-academy-id 986 :topic/title "Calculating the Equation of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation"}] :topic/id #uuid "dc19c9ce-6319-5943-8842-352e3c3735c7" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092083 :question/id #uuid "e405bf60-d500-55df-90be-a938dc9dfc93" :question/math-academy-id "e-6080" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=x^3 - x^2$ at the point $(1,0)$ is given by $y=px+q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the values of $p$ and $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^3 - x^2.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{x}^{2})=3{x}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1, 0){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=3-2=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm'=-\\dfrac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =-(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-1$ and $q=1.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092384 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091953 :question/id #uuid "4a734f55-ceac-587c-a831-fa8bed578a99" :question/math-academy-id "e-148" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y = 2x^3 -x^2$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x) = 2x^3-x^2.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\nf'(x)  =6x^2 -2x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = 6(1)^2 - 2(1) = 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line $m'$ is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac 1 4.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092385 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091954 :question/id #uuid "df59a07a-922c-50b9-8c2c-22ed57ac2bda" :question/math-academy-id "e-149" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x} -4x$ at the point $(1,-2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x}-4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{1/2}-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{-1/2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = (1)^{-1/2} - 4 = -3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = \\dfrac 1 3.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{1}{3}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(7,0).$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :topic/math-academy-id 987 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049470 :topic/math-academy-id 57 :topic/title "Implicit Differentiation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050162 :topic/math-academy-id 799 :topic/title "Calculating Tangent and Normal Lines with Parametric Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050960 :topic/math-academy-id 3172 :topic/title "Finding Derivatives Using a Graphing Calculator"}] :topic/title "Calculating the Equation of a Normal Line Using Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049195 :topic/math-academy-id 30 :topic/title "Combining the Laws of Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050321 :topic/math-academy-id 3769 :topic/title "The Sum Rule for Indefinite Integrals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690 :topic/math-academy-id 1116 :topic/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions"}] :topic/id #uuid "f580d418-6860-5b04-b964-2999aa08b42f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105573 :question/id #uuid "625c532a-d455-5b15-880d-e9fc5ae8ee72" :question/math-academy-id "e-374" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle\\int \\left(\\dfrac 2 x\\right)\\, \\textrm{d} x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $2$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\dfrac 1 x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105574 :question/id #uuid "9c57c69e-efa2-5e82-992c-53570e61af09" :question/math-academy-id "e-375" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\dfrac 1 {2x} + 3x$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{2x}+3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡|x|+3\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{ln⁡|x|}{2}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105575 :question/id #uuid "bf47e35e-da86-5f88-b1e7-0cc731c37b31" :question/math-academy-id "e-376" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\dfrac {3+2x} {x} ?$" :question/worked-solution "We separate the integrand into the sum of two fractions so that we can simplify each term. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+\\frac{2x}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{3}{x}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C =3 \\ln K$ where $K$ is some other constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $3\\ln|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3+2x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+C \\\\  & =3ln⁡|x|+2x+3ln⁡K \\\\  & =3ln⁡(K|x|)+2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1361 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557 :topic/math-academy-id 331 :topic/title "Integrating Linear Rational Functions Using Substitution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048754 :topic/math-academy-id 312 :topic/title "Integrating Exponential Functions"}] :topic/title "Integrating the Reciprocal Function"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050884 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213 :topic/math-academy-id 343 :topic/title "Solving Linear Equations With Variables on Both Sides"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051079 :topic/math-academy-id 87 :topic/title "Factoring Linear Expressions"}] :topic/id #uuid "6e36fc4f-7ad7-519b-88fa-4bc240d36c28" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203852 :question/id #uuid "fd07ad0f-548a-5569-84de-d658a88c4b9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-17087" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\nI. $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$\n\nII. $2z - 5 = z + 1$\n\nIII. $3z - 2 = 3z - 2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+3 & =4z+9 \\\\ 4z+3-4z & =4z+9-4z \\\\ 3 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-5 & =z+1 \\\\ 2z-5-z+5 & =z+1-z+5 \\\\ z & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2z - 5 = z + 1$ has the unique solution $z = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-2 & =3z-2 \\\\ 3z-2-3z & =3z-2-3z \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203853 :question/id #uuid "cba9196c-fdc3-594d-a1ad-948cf75bd366" :question/math-academy-id "e-17213" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n4(r - 2)y = -8\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay = b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 4(r - 2), \\qquad b = -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4(r - 2) = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(r-2) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4(r-2)}{4} & =\\frac{0}{4} \\\\ r-2 & =0 \\\\ r & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203878 :question/id #uuid "5ee14499-89b0-59a3-9f13-b0a00c0e518d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18853" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n(r - 9)z + 2 = -1\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-9)z+2 & =-1 \\\\ (r-9)z+2-2 & =-1-2 \\\\ {{(r-9)}_{⏟}}_{a}z & ={{-3}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr - 9 = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-9 & =0 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Constants on Both Sides"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204092 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203854 :question/id #uuid "36eadc0b-c2b0-5f84-8f39-2b8399932edb" :question/math-academy-id "e-17214" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2nx + 7  = -4x + 5\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+7 & =-4x+5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =-4x+5+4x \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+4x+7 & =5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7-7 & =5-7 \\\\ 2nx+4x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x  = -2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x  = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+4 & =0 \\\\ 2n+4-4 & =0-4 \\\\ 2n & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{2n}{2} & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\ n & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Variables and Constants on Both Sides"}] :topic/math-academy-id 5572 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048134 :topic/math-academy-id 6092 :topic/title "Equating Polynomial Coefficients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048576 :topic/math-academy-id 440 :topic/title "Rational Equations With Three Terms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050055 :topic/math-academy-id 6035 :topic/title "Determining the Number of Solutions to a Linear Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050240 :topic/math-academy-id 493 :topic/title "Systems of Equations With No Solutions and Infinitely Many Solutions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050981 :topic/math-academy-id 3992 :topic/title "Absolute Value Equations With Extraneous Solutions"}] :topic/title "Linear Equations With No Solutions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050420 :topic/math-academy-id 280 :topic/title "Differentiating Trigonometric Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690 :topic/math-academy-id 1116 :topic/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions"}] :topic/id #uuid "6fa843c3-0803-5725-a7d6-d567e4f1b216" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059677 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059505 :question/id #uuid "0bc72287-b782-5add-89ef-f2a465573ab0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3653" :question/problem "Find $f''(x)$ for $f(x) = 2x^3 + \\dfrac 1 x.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(2{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =2(3){x}^{2}-{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}-{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =(6{x}^{2}-{x}^{-2})^{′} \\\\  & =6(2)x-(-2){x}^{-3} \\\\  & =12x+2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059678 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059506 :question/id #uuid "b092bc79-d369-5ff0-a6bb-5fc059699275" :question/math-academy-id "e-3654" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for $y = 3 x^4 + 5x+ \\sqrt x.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+5x+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{4}+5x+{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =3(4){x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =12{x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(12{x}^{3}+5+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =12(3){x}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =36{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives Using Leibnitz Notation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059679 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059507 :question/id #uuid "627f1ef4-64a8-5764-89c7-cb4e31009dbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-3655" :question/problem "Given $y = \\dfrac {3 x^2 + x - 1} x$, find $\\dfrac {\\textrm d^{3} y} {\\textrm d x^3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as the sum of powers of $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}+x-1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =3x+1-{x}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x+1-{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =3+0-(-1){x}^{-2} \\\\  & =3+{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3+{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2){x}^{-3} \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the third derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-2(-3){x}^{-4} \\\\  & =6{x}^{-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing Higher Order Derivatives"}] :topic/math-academy-id 281 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048202 :topic/math-academy-id 650 :topic/title "Differential Operators"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048261 :topic/math-academy-id 3287 :topic/title "Calculating Moments Using Moment-Generating Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048713 :topic/math-academy-id 824 :topic/title "Calculating Acceleration for Straight-Line Motion Using Differentiation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048727 :topic/math-academy-id 431 :topic/title "Second Derivatives of Parametric Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048999 :topic/math-academy-id 4139 :topic/title "Differentiating Vector-Valued Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049019 :topic/math-academy-id 1177 :topic/title "Second-Degree Taylor Polynomials"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049070 :topic/math-academy-id 4386 :topic/title "Infinite Sets"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592 :topic/math-academy-id 3215 :topic/title "Introduction to Differential Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050120 :topic/math-academy-id 3846 :topic/title "Relating Concavity to the Second Derivative"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051036 :topic/math-academy-id 463 :topic/title "L'Hopital's Rule"}] :topic/title "Second and Higher-Order Derivatives"}]}
